{"@context":{"lean":"lean:","block":"block:","imports":{"@type":"@id"}},"@graph":[{"@id":"lean:module:QOU.BraidsAndKnots.ComponentCountSelectivity","@type":"LeanModule","name":"QOU.BraidsAndKnots.ComponentCountSelectivity","imports":["lean:module:Init","lean:module:Mathlib.Algebra.Order.Ring.Defs","lean:module:Mathlib.Algebra.Order.Ring.Abs","lean:module:Mathlib.Tactic.Ring","lean:module:Mathlib.Tactic.Linarith"],"declarations":9,"chunks":1},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidsAndKnots.ComponentCountSelectivity.proton_neutron_indistinguishable","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidsAndKnots.ComponentCountSelectivity.proton_neutron_indistinguishable","kind":"theorem","line":94,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidsAndKnots/ComponentCountSelectivity.lean#L94-L98","doc":"Specialised to the proton/neutron pair: any component-count function assigns\nthem the same value, because the framework gives both `c = 3`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidsAndKnots.ComponentCountSelectivity.no_separation_of_equal_component_count","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidsAndKnots.ComponentCountSelectivity.no_separation_of_equal_component_count","kind":"theorem","line":80,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidsAndKnots/ComponentCountSelectivity.lean#L80-L92","doc":"**The negative result, and it is the load-bearing one.**  A quantity that is\na function of the component count alone cannot separate two species that share\na component count.\n\nIn the framework's own assignments the proton and the neutron are both the\nBorromean linking of three quarks, so `c = 3` for each.  Hence no invariant of\nthis shape distinguishes them, and in particular the `q = 1` collapse cannot\nbe a charge-separation mechanism: it does not see charge.  Measured statement\nof the same fact, with the corpus citations for the assignments, in the probe\nnamed above. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidsAndKnots.ComponentCountSelectivity.jonesAtOne_ne_one_of_two_le","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidsAndKnots.ComponentCountSelectivity.jonesAtOne_ne_one_of_two_le","kind":"theorem","line":73,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidsAndKnots/ComponentCountSelectivity.lean#L73-L78","doc":"The invariant separates single-component from multi-component links. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidsAndKnots.ComponentCountSelectivity.jonesAtOne_eq_one_iff","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidsAndKnots.ComponentCountSelectivity.jonesAtOne_eq_one_iff","kind":"theorem","line":49,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidsAndKnots/ComponentCountSelectivity.lean#L49-L71","doc":"**The selectivity theorem.**  Among links, the `q = 1` invariant reaches the\nunknot's value `1` exactly when the link has a single component.\n\nThis is the mathematical content of \"the `q = 1` layer is transparent to\n`c = 1` objects and to nothing else\".  The forward direction is the one that\ndoes work: it says no multi-component link can hide at the interface. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidsAndKnots.ComponentCountSelectivity.abs_jonesAtOne","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidsAndKnots.ComponentCountSelectivity.abs_jonesAtOne","kind":"theorem","line":45,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidsAndKnots/ComponentCountSelectivity.lean#L45-L47","doc":"The magnitude grows without bound in `c`: `|V_L(1)| = 2^(c-1)`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidsAndKnots.ComponentCountSelectivity.jonesAtOne_three","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidsAndKnots.ComponentCountSelectivity.jonesAtOne_three","kind":"theorem","line":42,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidsAndKnots/ComponentCountSelectivity.lean#L42-L43","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidsAndKnots.ComponentCountSelectivity.jonesAtOne_two","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidsAndKnots.ComponentCountSelectivity.jonesAtOne_two","kind":"theorem","line":39,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidsAndKnots/ComponentCountSelectivity.lean#L39-L40","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidsAndKnots.ComponentCountSelectivity.jonesAtOne_one","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidsAndKnots.ComponentCountSelectivity.jonesAtOne_one","kind":"theorem","line":36,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidsAndKnots/ComponentCountSelectivity.lean#L36-L37","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidsAndKnots.ComponentCountSelectivity.jonesAtOne","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidsAndKnots.ComponentCountSelectivity.jonesAtOne","kind":"def","line":30,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidsAndKnots/ComponentCountSelectivity.lean#L30-L34","doc":"The `q = 1` value of the Jones polynomial, as a function of the component\ncount `c ≥ 1`.  This is a DEFINITION of the quantity whose behaviour we study,\nnot a claim that the Jones polynomial equals it; that is Jones' theorem and is\ncited, not reproved. ","signatureChunk":0}]}